// ============================================================================
// flat-query.dax : the measure floor probe
// ============================================================================
//
// WHAT IT PROVES
//   This runs the SAME base measures that the matrix's dispatch measure
//   switches to, one measure per column, with NO dispatch axis on rows. The
//   elapsed time is the floor. No visual technique goes below it: a bridge
//   table, a native restructure and a Deneb grid all still have to evaluate
//   these measures.
//
//   Timed against the dispatch query under the SAME pinned filters, it gives
//   you the only two numbers that decide the fix:
//
//     dispatch overhead = dispatch query - flat query   what a structural fix can win
//     measure floor     = flat query                    what no fix can win
//
//   If the flat query is already most of the dispatch query, STOP optimising
//   the visual. Go optimise the measures, or put fewer of them on screen.
//   Worked example from the field: 1,997 ms warm dispatch against a 1,500 ms
//   warm flat query is roughly 500 ms of dispatch sitting on a 1,500 ms
//   measure floor, so a structural fix alone was never going to make that
//   visual fast.
//
//   Second reading, free: run it at least twice. Warm close to cold means
//   formula-engine bound (plan construction, not scanning). Warm much faster
//   than cold means the scans are real and caching helps. Supporting signal
//   only. Warm close to cold confirms dispatch; warm much faster than cold
//   does NOT rule it out. Only the flat-versus-dispatch comparison settles it.
//
// TWO WAYS TO GET THIS WRONG
//   1. Not pinning the slicers. An unpinned query runs against a wider or
//      emptier filter context and gives you a number with no relationship to
//      the visual you are trying to explain. Every filter below is mandatory.
//   2. Dropping the totals. If the matrix shows a total column or subtotal
//      rows, the flat query needs the matching ALLSELECTED total measures or
//      you have timed a different visual. On the field model, deleting the 36
//      total columns took 1,580 ms to 819 ms cold: half the columns, roughly
//      half the time, and a meaningless comparison.
//
// ============================================================================

EVALUATE
CALCULATETABLE (
    SUMMARIZECOLUMNS (

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------
        // GROUP BY: every axis the matrix has EXCEPT the dispatch column.
        // 'Statement Rows'[Items] (or whatever the SWITCH reads with
        // SELECTEDVALUE) must NOT appear here. Removing it is the point.
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------
        'Dim Business Unit'[Business Unit],

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------
        // THE BASE MEASURES the dispatch measure switches to. One column
        // each, in the same order as the matrix rows. Do not write new
        // measures for this: the whole value of the probe is that it uses
        // the measures that are already there.
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------
        "Revenue",              [Revenue],
        "Cost of Sales",        [Cost of Sales],
        "Gross Profit",         [Gross Profit],

        // The comparison slot, if the matrix has one (Var LY, Budget, ...).
        "Revenue PY",           [Revenue PY],
        "Cost of Sales PY",     [Cost of Sales PY],
        "Gross Profit PY",      [Gross Profit PY],

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------
        // THE TOTALS. Include these whenever the matrix shows a total
        // column or a subtotal row. One per base measure above.
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------
        "Revenue Total",        CALCULATE ( [Revenue],        ALLSELECTED ( 'Dim Business Unit' ) ),
        "Cost of Sales Total",  CALCULATE ( [Cost of Sales],  ALLSELECTED ( 'Dim Business Unit' ) ),
        "Gross Profit Total",   CALCULATE ( [Gross Profit],   ALLSELECTED ( 'Dim Business Unit' ) )
    ),

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    // PINNED CONTEXT: one line per slicer that is set on the page, matching
    // the screenshot you are trying to explain. The dispatch query you
    // compare against must carry these same filters, unchanged.
    //
    // If the engine refuses SUMMARIZECOLUMNS inside CALCULATETABLE (older
    // builds do), pass these as filter-table arguments to SUMMARIZECOLUMNS
    // instead. That is what Performance Analyzer's Copy query emits anyway.
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    'Dim Calendar'[Fiscal Year] = 2025,
    'Dim Scenario'[Scenario]    = "Actual"
)

// ============================================================================
// ADAPTING THIS
//   1. Open the dispatch measure. List every measure its SWITCH returns.
//      Those are your columns. Section-header branches that return a literal
//      0 contribute nothing and can be left out.
//   2. Replace the group-by column with the matrix's non-dispatch axis. If
//      the matrix has no other axis, drop the group-by entirely and return a
//      single row: SUMMARIZECOLUMNS ( "Revenue", [Revenue], ... ).
//   3. Copy the pinned filters verbatim from the dispatch query.
//   4. Save the dispatch query beside this one and time BOTH:
//        run_dax.ps1 -QueryFile .\dispatch.dax -Runs 2 -Rows 0
//        run_dax.ps1 -QueryFile .\flat-query.dax -Runs 2 -Rows 0
//      Same session, same slicer state, back to back.
//   5. Optional narrowing: cut the column list to the handful of additive
//      base measures and re-time. On the field model the 7 additive measures
//      cost 192 ms cold / 61 ms warm against 819 ms / 838 ms for all 36, so
//      777 ms warm was traceable to the non-additive columns. That tells you
//      WHICH measures are the floor, not just that the floor exists.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT MEASURE
//   The per-cell tax. An EVALUATE never evaluates formatStringDefinition and
//   never runs a conditional-formatting colour measure, so this query is
//   structurally blind to both. That gap is what Performance Analyzer sees
//   and this does not. See references/per-cell-tax.md.
// ============================================================================
